\author{Compiled by Claude Sonnet 5 (claude-sonnet-5) for Andr\'as Kornai}
\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
North Tanna (ISO 639-3 tnn; Glottocode nort2847) is an Austronesian language spoken on the northern coast of Tanna Island, Vanuatu, with about 5,000 speakers. Its relation to neighbouring Tanna languages such as Whitesands and Lenakel is not settled. The search found very little specific to North Tanna.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks or school programmes were found. The reference description says the language is taught as a subject in some schools, but I found no source for this.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item North Tanna language (encyclopedia overview): \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Tanna_language}
\item Languages of Tanna: \url{https://nthieberger.net/vanlangs/TANNA.HTM}
\item Cambridge Language Centre page on the Tanna languages: \url{https://www.langcen.cam.ac.uk/resources/langt/tanna.html}
\item Whitesands language, a close relative: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whitesands_language}
\item Hammond (2009), \emph{The Grammar of Nouns and Verbs in Whitesands} (a related language, not North Tanna): \url{https://ses.library.usyd.edu.au/handle/2123/5796?show=full}
\end{itemize}
A 2018 Uppsala University master's thesis, ``A grammar sketch of North Tanna'' by Kristin Sverredal, was reported in the search summary. No link to it was seen, so none is given. No North Tanna dictionary was found.

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None found.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
None found.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Documentation is thin. The Sverredal grammar sketch is the only North Tanna description reported. Related-language materials for Lenakel and Whitesands could inform an orthography, but they are not North Tanna and should be treated with care.
\end{document}
